    "plainText": "392 I know this, myself. I was standing down there. And you\u0027d think the golden jubilee was on, you hear the bells ringing. And here a poor little woman, coming down the street, dragging her feet. And a father packing a baby; and two or three of them, crying. She was doing penance to some dead woman up there. Had her\u2026Thought she\u0027d get to go to Heaven, by it. Oh, what a pitiful thing!\n\n393 Then I seen, standing down there, here come\u2026Their economics are so poorly balanced! The church takes everything they got. Here, a little Pancho, maybe\u2014maybe Pancho mean Frank. He comes down, and he\u0027s a brick mason, and he make\u2014he makes twenty pesos a week. But it\u0027ll take the whole twenty pesos, buy him a pair of shoes. That\u0027s their economics. But now, here, what about then if\u2014if he, being a mason and a brick layer, and make twenty pesos a week, just saying. I don\u0027t know what he makes, but say that kind of an economical way it\u0027s balanced up. Notice, now, if he makes twenty pesos a week.\n\n",
